The challenge of defining effectively no snow
                        Adrienne M. Marshall 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-5555-25481 & Timothy E. Link2

Nature Reviews Earth & Environment
                             (2026) Cite this article









            Save article




                View saved research

Siirila-Woodburn et al. define snowpack conditions based on percentiles of historical snow water equivalent (SWE)1. Specifically, ‘low snow’ is defined as “conditions in which peak SWE falls between the 30th and 10th percentiles”, and ‘effectively no snow’ is defined as “peak SWE conditions within the lowest 10th percentile”. This definition of ‘low-to-no-snow’ conditions is described as an important metric to help stakeholders in snow-dependent systems develop proactive adaptation strategies for water resources and ecosystem management.As applied in Siirila-Woodburn et al., the ‘effectively no snow’ definition presupposes that over the historical range of variability (HRV), each location has effectively no snow at its lowest 10% of years. However, in much of the western United States (WUS), this definition is unlikely to match colloquial or scientific understandings of ‘effectively no snow’, as observations meeting this criterion could be snow-covered, with sufficient SWE to contribute meaningfully to water resources.

